menu "LagunBus — dial"

config LAGUN_TWAI_TX
    int "TWAI transmit GPIO"
    default 4
    help
        4 and 5 by default, because on the ESP32-C3 Super Mini they are broken out
        and play no other role. AVOID 2, 8 and 9 — those are boot-mode strapping
        pins, and their state at reset decides whether the board starts at all.
        Avoid 18 and 19 too: USB Serial/JTAG goes that way, that is, flashing and
        the log.

config LAGUN_TWAI_RX
    int "TWAI receive GPIO"
    default 5

config LAGUN_ENCODER_A_GPIO
    int "Encoder channel A GPIO"
    default 0
    help
        The encoder's A contact, shorting to its common pin C, which goes to ground;
        the internal pull-up holds it high between. An interrupt on both edges.
        0, 1 and 3 by default: the whole encoder on the row with GND, five wires in one
        place of the breadboard. GPIO0 and GPIO1 are the pins of a 32 kHz crystal the
        Super Mini does not have (the RTC runs on its internal RC), and no strapping
        pins; GPIO2, between them and 3, is one — left free.

config LAGUN_ENCODER_B_GPIO
    int "Encoder channel B GPIO"
    default 1

config LAGUN_ENCODER_STEPS_PER_DETENT
    int "Gray code transitions per detent"
    default 4
    range 1 4
    help
        How many transitions of A and B one click of the knob makes. An EC11 makes 4
        (a full cycle between two detents); some make 2. Too many and a click does
        nothing until the next; too few and one click is two steps. Hardware, not an
        attribute: the hub sees steps, never transitions (zadania/devices/DEVICE-02.md N3).

config LAGUN_ENCODER_REVERSE
    bool "Clockwise turns the value down"
    default n
    help
        Yes when A and B are wired the other way round — the same as swapping the
        two wires.

config LAGUN_KEY_GPIO
    int "Shaft key GPIO"
    default 3
    help
        The key in the encoder's shaft (SW), shorting to ground, with the internal
        pull-up. main.c reads a LOW pin as a press.

config LAGUN_SERVICE_GPIO
    int "Service button GPIO"
    default 9
    help
        The service button: held 5 s and let go, a factory reset by the device itself
        (devices/README.md). 9 by default — BOOT on the Super Mini, wired to ground; an
        external button goes from GPIO9 to GND next to it. Held at power-on it enters
        the download mode instead, which harms nothing: let go and power up again.

config LAGUN_STATUS_LED_GPIO
    int "Status LED GPIO"
    default 8
    help
        The status LED: what the node is doing (devices/README.md). 8 by default — the
        on-board LED of the Super Mini; an external one goes from 3V3 through a resistor
        to GPIO8. GPIO8 is a strapping pin and must not be pulled to ground at reset.

config LAGUN_STATUS_LED_ACTIVE_LOW
    bool "Status LED lights on a LOW level"
    default y
    help
        Yes for the Super Mini's on-board LED and for an external one wired from 3V3.

config LAGUN_BITRATE
    int "Bus speed [bit/s]"
    default 500000
    help
        Has to agree with the hub and with EVERY other node. A node at a different
        speed not only hears nobody — it corrupts frames for all the others.

endmenu
